heading('Abstract',1); p('This project presents the design and development of an Integrated Healthcare Service Management Web Application. The application centralises common patient-facing and administrative activities, including registration, appointment scheduling, queue management, provider discovery, consultation support, pharmacy ordering, laboratory bookings, records, reminders, family-member management, notifications and reporting. It is a responsive web application; a mobile application is outside the scope of the present implementation. The implemented application uses Python and Flask with Jinja templates, SQLAlchemy, a local SQLite database configuration, Bootstrap-based responsive presentation and a Vercel deployment configuration. The codebase also contains support for authentication, session management, language preferences, JSON-backed translation catalogues and selected real-time communication components.\n\nThe study adopts an Agile development approach to allow the modules to be developed and reviewed iteratively. The report documents requirements, architecture, data entities, interfaces, verification activities and deployment considerations. Features described as available in source code are distinguished from configuration-dependent, simulated or future integrations. In particular, the conversational helper and symptom-checking components provide general informational guidance only; they do not diagnose conditions, prescribe treatment or replace consultation with a qualified healthcare professional. Full-language coverage remains a verification requirement: every visible string, form message, validation message, notification and chatbot text must be audited in each supported language before release. The project demonstrates how a modular web platform can improve access to coordinated healthcare-service information while retaining clear operational and clinical-safety boundaries.')
