chapter('CHAPTER ONE: INTRODUCTION')
heading('1.1 Background of the Study',2); p('Healthcare service delivery involves recurring coordination tasks: identifying a provider, reserving a suitable time, arriving at the facility, monitoring a queue, obtaining follow-up information and managing related services. When these activities are disconnected, patients may need to repeat information or use separate channels for appointments, pharmacy requests, laboratory services and support. Digital health systems can help organise these interactions when they are designed around people, workflows, privacy and local clinical governance. The World Health Organization identifies digital health as an important part of health-system transformation, while also recognising that implementation must be guided by appropriate strategy, resources and governance [1].\n\nThe project responds to this context through a single responsive web application. It offers role-based patient, provider and administrator workflows and consolidates service-related tasks into a shared interface. The application is intended as a service-management platform, not an electronic substitute for clinical judgement. Medical decisions remain the responsibility of qualified professionals and approved institutional processes.')
heading('1.2 Problem Statement',2); p('Patients can experience difficulty coordinating appointments, clinic queues, provider information, follow-up tasks and supporting services through fragmented manual or disconnected digital processes. Service staff likewise require timely visibility of bookings, queues, users, inventory-related requests and announcements. A web-based platform that brings these functions together can improve information access and workflow visibility; however, it must do so without overstating automation, compromising personal data or presenting informational AI outputs as clinical advice.')
heading('1.3 Aim of the Study',2); p('The aim is to design and develop an integrated healthcare service management web application that provides a coordinated, accessible and role-based interface for healthcare-service activities.')
heading('1.4 Objectives of the Study',2)
for x in ['Develop secure account registration, login and role-based access workflows.','Provide appointment booking, rescheduling, cancellation and provider-availability functions.','Support queue joining, status management and estimated-wait visibility.','Provide provider directory, profile and review functions.','Integrate service modules for records, laboratory requests, pharmacy requests, reminders, family members, notifications and billing records.','Provide information-oriented symptom and conversational helper interfaces with explicit clinical-safety limitations.','Implement multilingual presentation so that all user-facing text is translated consistently across supported interfaces.','Provide administrative capabilities for users, providers, queues, pharmacy stock, announcements and analytics.','Deploy the web application using the project’s Vercel configuration and maintain the source using Git and GitHub.']: bullet(x)
heading('1.5 Significance of the Study',2); p('For patients, the platform provides a single point of access to service information and self-service tasks. For providers and administrators, it provides structured views of bookings, queues and selected operational records. For the project, it demonstrates practical application of web engineering concepts including authentication, database modelling, responsive design, localisation and deployment. Its multilingual approach is an accessibility feature because it seeks to make the complete interface—not merely navigation labels—understandable to users with different language preferences.')
heading('1.6 Scope of the Study',2); p('The scope is a web application. Source-code evidence supports modules for user accounts; patient, doctor and administrator roles; appointments and slots; queues; profiles; records; laboratory bookings; pharmacy catalogue and orders; billing records; family members; reminders; vaccinations; insurance profiles; emergency alerts; chat consultations; reviews; notifications; announcements; analytics; and language preferences. The project includes Vercel configuration for web deployment. A dedicated native mobile application, live clinical-device integration, a production payment-gateway integration and a production-grade generative-AI integration are not evidenced by the inspected repository and are therefore outside the confirmed implementation scope.')
heading('1.7 Limitations of the Study',2); p('The application depends on network access and correct server-side configuration. The checked payment endpoint marks an appointment as paid and generates a reference in application data; no external payment-provider integration is evidenced and it must not be represented as live financial processing. Email delivery depends on SMTP environment variables. The configured local database is SQLite, while Vercel serverless storage requires an externally configured database for durable production data. Translation files exist for supported locales, but completeness of every text string requires a release audit. AI-labelled helper functions are rule-based/informational in the inspected source and require validation, escalation rules and governance before any clinical deployment.')
heading('1.8 Organization of the Report',2); p('Chapter One introduces the project. Chapter Two reviews related digital-health platforms and the identified gap. Chapter Three describes requirements and methodology. Chapter Four presents the design and implementation. Chapter Five defines testing and evaluation. Chapter Six concludes the report and proposes future work.')

chapter('CHAPTER TWO: LITERATURE REVIEW')
heading('2.1 Introduction',2); p('This chapter situates the proposed application within digital healthcare and patient-service management. It uses public, official sources for platform descriptions and avoids feature claims that cannot be supported by those sources.')
heading('2.2 Digital Healthcare Systems',2); p('Digital healthcare systems use information and communication technologies to support health-related information, service delivery and management. Their value depends on more than a user interface: appropriate governance, privacy safeguards, usable workflows, accessible language and integration with responsible clinical processes are essential. WHO’s global strategy frames digital health as a means to support health outcomes and health-system priorities, not as a stand-alone solution [1].')
heading('2.3 Healthcare Service Management Systems',2); p('Healthcare service management systems coordinate operational interactions such as scheduling, check-in, queue visibility, communication and service requests. A well-designed system separates access control by role, maintains an auditable data model and presents task-focused interfaces. It should also communicate limitations clearly: operational convenience does not itself confirm medical accuracy or clinical appropriateness.')
heading('2.4 Existing Healthcare Applications',2); p('MyChart is a patient portal product with documented support for scheduling and managing appointments, provider discovery, family access, medical-record viewing/sharing, billing-related functions and virtual-care options, subject to the participating organisation’s configuration [2–5]. Practo and Apollo 24/7 are relevant regional consumer-health platforms; the features available to a user can vary by geography, provider, service category and current product configuration. Their exact comparable functionality should be verified against the official pages applicable to the project’s intended deployment country before final submission. This study uses them as contextual examples rather than asserting an exhaustive feature set.')
heading('2.5 Comparison of Existing Systems',2)
table(['Table 2.1: System','Appointments','Records / family','Virtual care','Pharmacy / lab','Evidence note'],[['MyChart','Documented','Documented','Documented','Configuration-dependent','Official MyChart feature pages [2–5].'],['Practo','[VERIFY FOR TARGET MARKET]','[VERIFY]','[VERIFY]','[VERIFY]','Verify current official pages before final submission.'],['Apollo 24/7','[VERIFY FOR TARGET MARKET]','[VERIFY]','[VERIFY]','[VERIFY]','Verify current official pages before final submission.'],['Proposed system','Implemented booking, reschedule/cancel routes','Implemented records and family-member entities/routes','Telemedicine and chat modules present','Pharmacy and lab modules present','Repository inspection; external fulfilment/integration not confirmed.']],[1.1,1.0,1.1,1.1,2.6])
heading('2.6 Limitations/Gaps in Existing Systems',2); p('A platform may be comprehensive within a particular healthcare network yet not provide a unified experience across independently managed services. Conversely, a broad consumer platform may not match the workflows, language needs or governance requirements of a local setting. The gap addressed by this project is a configurable, role-based web interface that gathers appointment, queue, service-request, communication and accessibility functions in a coherent design. It is not a claim that the project replaces mature clinical information systems or their regulatory controls.')
heading('2.7 Proposed Solution',2); p('The proposed solution is an integrated web application with distinct patient, provider and administrator interfaces. Its data model links user accounts to provider information, appointment and queue records, service requests, reminders and notifications. Multilingual preferences are stored per patient and JSON translation catalogues are used to render translated interface text. The solution also reserves clear boundaries for integrations that require external authority: payment processing, durable cloud database, clinical records interoperability and clinically validated AI are configuration or future-work matters rather than confirmed built-in services.')
heading('2.8 Summary',2); p('The review supports an integrated approach to patient-service coordination. The proposed platform focuses on coherent web workflows, operational transparency and multilingual accessibility while retaining the distinction between software assistance and professional healthcare delivery.')

chapter('CHAPTER THREE: SYSTEM ANALYSIS AND METHODOLOGY')
heading('3.1 Introduction',2); p('This chapter defines the system’s users, requirements and development approach. Requirements are derived from the inspected repository and the project brief; items needing operational confirmation are explicitly marked.')
heading('3.2 Development Methodology',2); p('Agile development is suitable because the application contains interdependent modules that benefit from incremental delivery and feedback. A practical cycle is: prioritise a workflow; model its data; implement the route and template; test permissions and error paths; review usability; and refine the backlog. For this project, early increments can cover authentication, appointments and queues; later increments can cover records, pharmacy, laboratory, communication, localisation and administration. Each iteration should end with demonstrable acceptance criteria and a record of unresolved risks.')
heading('3.3 Requirement Analysis',2); p('Stakeholder needs were organised into patient access, provider workflow, administration, safety, privacy, accessibility and deployment. The key analysis principle is traceability: each requirement should map to an interface, route or data entity, and later to a test case. Requirements labelled “confirm” should be validated against the deployed application, not inferred from a template or mock-up.')
heading('3.4 Functional Requirements',2)
table(['ID','Requirement','Priority','Evidence / acceptance criterion'],[['FR-01','Register, authenticate and log out users by role.','High','Registration/login routes; role checks; valid credentials create session.'],['FR-02','Manage provider profiles and availability slots.','High','Doctor and AppointmentSlot entities; provider routes.'],['FR-03','Book, reschedule, cancel and view appointments.','High','Appointment routes; status and payment fields.'],['FR-04','Join and manage a consultation queue.','High','QueueRecord entity and queue action routes.'],['FR-05','Request pharmacy and laboratory services.','High','PharmacyMedicine/Order and LabTestBooking modules.'],['FR-06','Maintain records, reminders, family members and notifications.','High','Corresponding models/routes.'],['FR-07','Provide informational symptom and chat assistance.','Medium','AI helper routes; must show clinical disclaimer.'],['FR-08','Translate all visible user-facing content.','High','Language preferences and translation catalogues; verify coverage.'],['FR-09','Provide administrative user, provider, queue, pharmacy, announcement and analytics views.','High','Admin routes/templates and analytics route.']])
heading('3.5 Non-Functional Requirements',2)
table(['ID','Requirement','Measure / verification'],[['NFR-01','Security','Password hashing, authenticated routes and role checks are present; conduct security review before release.'],['NFR-02','Usability','Responsive Bootstrap interface; task testing with representative users.'],['NFR-03','Performance','Common pages should load within [INSERT TARGET] under [INSERT TEST LOAD].'],['NFR-04','Availability','Vercel deployment configuration; confirm uptime/monitoring arrangement.'],['NFR-05','Accessibility','Keyboard, contrast, labels and complete multilingual coverage to be tested.'],['NFR-06','Maintainability','Flask routes, Jinja templates and SQLAlchemy models are separated.'],['NFR-07','Privacy','Least-privilege access, secure configuration and data-retention policy require confirmation.']])
heading('3.6 System Users/Roles',2); p('Patient/User: registers, manages profile and family members, books appointments, joins queues, requests services, reviews records, receives notifications and accesses informational tools.\n\nHealthcare Provider: manages availability, views appropriate appointments and queue entries, records consultation-related notes where authorised, and participates in configured consultation workflows.\n\nAdministrator: manages users, providers, walk-ins, pharmacy items, announcements, queue actions and analytics. Administrative access must be restricted and audited in a production deployment.')
heading('3.7 Hardware Requirements',2); table(['Component','Minimum','Recommended'],[['Client device','Modern browser-capable computer','Current desktop/laptop with stable internet'],['Server/runtime','Vercel-compatible Python serverless runtime','Managed production environment with monitoring'],['Storage','Development SQLite file','Managed persistent database for production'],['Network','Internet connection','Reliable broadband with TLS-enabled deployment']])
heading('3.8 Software Requirements',2); p('Confirmed repository technologies: Python; Flask 3.0.3; Jinja2; Flask-SQLAlchemy/SQLAlchemy; Flask-Login; Flask-Babel/Babel; Bootstrap 5.1.3 loaded by template; Font Awesome loaded by template; optional Flask-SocketIO/socket.io client support; PyJWT; SQLite in the default local configuration; optional PostgreSQL when `DATABASE_URL` is supplied; Git and GitHub; and Vercel via `vercel.json`. [INSERT CONFIRM PYTHON VERSION USED FOR SUBMISSION.]')
heading('3.9 Use Case Analysis',2); p('The primary use cases are: register/login; browse providers; book/reschedule/cancel appointment; join/check queue; request laboratory or pharmacy service; view records and reminders; manage family member data; receive notifications; conduct configured chat/telemedicine workflow; and administer users, providers, queues, stock, announcements and reports. Each use case is constrained by authenticated role and ownership checks.')
heading('3.10 Use Case Diagram',2); placeholder('[INSERT USE CASE DIAGRAM HERE]','Figure 3.1: Use Case Diagram','Show Patient, Healthcare Provider and Administrator actors, with their principal interactions and authenticated boundaries.')
heading('3.11 System Flow',2); p('A patient registers and signs in, then selects a provider or service. For appointments, the application retrieves available slots, validates the request and stores an appointment with status information. A patient may later reschedule, cancel, pay through the currently implemented internal payment-status flow, or check in/join a queue where permitted. Staff and administrators update relevant records; notifications and dashboard summaries present the current state. Language selection is resolved from the authenticated preference or session/cookie setting, then the application retrieves translations from the selected catalogue. Sensitive functions should reject unauthorised requests and log relevant operational events [INSERT CONFIRM LOGGING POLICY].')
heading('3.12 Summary',2); p('The analysis establishes a modular, role-based system whose critical requirements are appointment coordination, queue visibility, service access, multilingual accessibility and safe information boundaries. Agile iterations support progressively verifying these requirements.')

chapter('CHAPTER FOUR: SYSTEM DESIGN AND IMPLEMENTATION')
heading('4.1 Introduction',2); p('This chapter describes the confirmed implementation structure and the intended user interfaces. Screenshots must be replaced with genuine captures of the deployed or locally running application, annotated only after their content has been verified.')
heading('4.2 System Architecture',2); p('The application follows a server-rendered web architecture. A browser sends HTTP requests to Flask routes. Flask applies authentication and role checks, renders Jinja templates and persists data through SQLAlchemy. The default non-serverless configuration uses SQLite; the code accepts a PostgreSQL `DATABASE_URL` when supplied. Static assets and JSON translation files support the interface. A Vercel configuration builds `app.py` using `@vercel/python` and routes requests to the application. Socket.IO imports and client assets are present for chat-oriented features, but production real-time behaviour should be validated in the Vercel environment before it is claimed.')
placeholder('[INSERT SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM HERE]','Figure 4.1: System Architecture','Show Browser → Vercel/Flask → Authentication/Business Modules → SQLAlchemy → Persistent Database; include translation assets and optional SMTP/real-time services only if configured.')
heading('4.3 Database Design',2); p('SQLAlchemy models represent the main domain concepts. User is the central identity entity. Doctor extends provider information through a user relationship. Appointments connect patients, doctors, optional family members and payment fields; queue records connect patients and doctors. Supporting entities retain service, communication and health-management data. Foreign keys create explicit ownership and association boundaries.')
placeholder('[INSERT ER DIAGRAM HERE]','Figure 4.2: Entity Relationship Diagram','Include User, Doctor, Appointment, QueueRecord, AppointmentSlot, FamilyMember, HealthRecord, LabTestBooking, PharmacyMedicine, PharmacyOrder, ChatConsultation, ChatMessage, Notification and other implemented entities.')
heading('4.4 Database Tables',2)
table(['Table 4.1: Entity','Purpose','Key relationships'],[['User','Credentials, identity, role and profile information.','One-to-one Doctor where applicable; one-to-many patient records.'],['Doctor','Provider specialty, fee, biography, qualifications and telemedicine flag.','Belongs to User; has appointments, slots, queues and reviews.'],['Appointment','Scheduled care request, status, consultation and payment fields.','Patient User; Doctor; optional FamilyMember.'],['QueueRecord','Walk-in/consultation queue state and estimated wait.','Patient User and Doctor.'],['AppointmentSlot','Provider availability interval.','Doctor.'],['HealthRecord','Patient-uploaded record metadata and optional sharing link.','Patient User; optional Doctor.'],['LabTestBooking','Laboratory test request and status.','Patient User.'],['PharmacyMedicine / PharmacyOrder','Catalogue/inventory metadata and patient medicine order.','Order belongs to patient and medicine.'],['FamilyMember','Dependent details managed by a patient.','Patient User; optional appointments.'],['ChatConsultation / ChatMessage','Consultation topic, state and messages.','Patient, optional Doctor and sender User.'],['Notification / Announcement','Targeted messages and administrator-created announcements.','Notification recipient User; announcement creator User.'],['LanguagePreference','Persisted language and voice-assistance preference.','Patient User.']])
heading('4.5 User Interface Design',2); p('The interface uses reusable Jinja templates and Bootstrap to provide a responsive layout. Usability principles include clear page titles, task-oriented navigation, feedback messages, labelled controls, consistent status indicators and role-appropriate dashboards. All visible strings should pass through the project translation approach. The quality requirement is full interface localisation: content, headings, forms, controls, confirmations, errors, validations, notifications, footer, FAQ and chatbot interface—not navigation alone—must switch with the selected language.')

